-# Ideation organism — an automated research pole that cannot grade its own homework
+# Ideation organism — an automated research pole built so that no agent grades its own homework
 
-**What it is.** A small, fully inspectable system in which language-model agents from several providers propose research hypotheses on AI control, and in which *none of the agents that produce an idea is allowed to decide whether it survives*. Ideas are scored by a frozen statistical judge against sealed predictions, screened by a gatekeeper that only looks at their *form*, checked for novelty, killed by preregistered "death tests" run in a sandbox, and selected from one generation to the next. Every artefact is hash-sealed before it is read; every decisive number is traced to a result file.
+**What it is.** A small, fully inspectable system in which language-model agents from several providers propose research hypotheses on AI control, and in which, following a principle already published (see Related work below), *none of the agents that produce an idea is allowed to decide whether it survives*. Ideas are scored by a frozen statistical judge against sealed predictions, screened by a gatekeeper that only looks at their *form*, checked for novelty, killed by preregistered "death tests" run in a sandbox, and selected from one generation to the next. Every artefact is hash-sealed before it is read; every decisive number is traced to a result file.
 
 **Why it exists.** Automated researchers are useful only if they resist the two ways an optimiser games a research loop: *grading itself* (the proposer, the judge and the experimenter share a mind) and *drifting into the familiar* (the loop rewards what the judge already likes). This repository is one concrete answer, built and audited over October 2026 by a single researcher with AI coding agents under written rules, and it is published so that others can read the guards, run the offline demonstrations and try to break them.
 
-**Related work.** Closest to the judge is POPPER (Huang, Jin, Li, Li, Candès, Leskovec, "Automated hypothesis validation with agentic sequential falsifications", ICML 2025, PMLR 267:25372–25437; arXiv:2502.09858), where an e-value rule with a 1/α threshold, written outside the agents, already decides on free-form hypotheses produced by language models: using e-values and such a threshold is therefore not claimed here. What is claimed is narrower: the statistic is computed by the orchestrator, outside every agent, from a commitment sealed before the data exist, against a null formed by the known laws (the archive), with multiple comparisons controlled. The principle of the cheaters bench (section 3.1) and of the Goodhart attack (section 4) — an adaptive analyst who fools a reused holdout set; an attack through the scores a leaderboard returns, and nothing else — has been prior art since 2015 (Dwork, Feldman, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Roth, "Generalization in Adaptive Data Analysis and Holdout Reuse", arXiv:1506.02629; Blum and Hardt, "The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions", arXiv:1502.04585); only their form is claimed: a language-model proposer that reads the exact description of a statistical judge and, in one arm, its verdicts, on worlds where every promotion is false. That form was not found in the work read so far; the papers that cite POPPER remain to be read. As a loop that generates, ranks and evolves ideas, the system has a neighbour in Co-Scientist (Gottweis et al., Nature 655, 487–496, 2026; preprint "Towards an AI co-scientist", arXiv:2502.18864).
+**Related work.** The principles listed below with their references are already published, and none of them is claimed here; this repository adopts them and cites them.
 
-**Status (13 November 2026).** Pre-pilot stage. One full ideation round has been run with real agents (29 idea cards, 17 distinct ideas). The statistical judge has been exercised on a real bank of model episodes, on planted worlds, against 11 scripted cheaters (decisive validation, section 3.1) and against a language-model proposer that reads its exact description and sees its verdicts (Goodhart attack, clean rerun of 5 November, section 4). That attack found the judge's one declared gap; a new null (v9) that closes it has a written, counter-read proof, and its decisive validation, sealed on 3 November, returned "power lost", the outcome it expected in writing: v8 remains the production null until the project lead decides on that price. Attacked in turn as a candidate (verdict of 10 November; [status page](resultats/attaque-goodhart-v2/)), v9 "resists (at this size)" on the world inside its null, and at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, the outcome is "frontier crossed, robust": the proposer finds a law that replays above α where the archive alone does not pass (10 of the 17 laws promoted under v9 replay above α after Holm). 8 of 11 predictions held; reservations and open decisions on the status page. The death-test stage and the generation-to-generation loop have now run for real once, at the smallest size: after two runs of 12 November 2026 that stopped at the first proposer call ("not judgeable (stop)", code v66, sealed text v1), the third attempt under sealed text v2 (code v67, configuration v8) played both generations, and a fresh reader confirmed outcome C1, "the chain holds (covered class, this world, this size)": class covered by the v8 null, toy world S-V1, one and a half generations; nothing about the uncovered class, real data or the quality of the ideas; seven reservations, none changing the outcome; always reported with the out-of-class breach of the Goodhart attack ([status page](resultats/course-v67/)). Nothing here is a validated scientific result about AI control; it is an instrument, with its test suite, its mutation bench and its known gaps stated below.
+- *Separating who proposes from who judges.* Qu, Chen, Wang ("Propose, Don't Judge: An Anytime-Valid Referee for LLM Agents That Mine Investment Factors", arXiv:2609.27051v1, 2026) separate the proposing agent from the referee that decides (pp. 1, 2, 4, 11) and claim the priority of that separation (pp. 2, 4); PROCTOR (Wahi, "LLM-as-a-Judge Is Not an Oracle: Why Self-Improving Agents Need Deterministic Guardrails", arXiv:2609.02246v1, 2026) also forbids any component from both proposing and applying, or both judging and acting (p. 4), and describes a silent fallback as a failure (pp. 1, 7).
+- *A statistic computed outside the agents, by trusted code.* POPPER (Huang, Jin, Li, Li, Candès, Leskovec, "Automated hypothesis validation with agentic sequential falsifications", ICML 2025, PMLR 267:25372–25437; arXiv:2502.09858) already decides on free-form hypotheses produced by language models with an e-value rule and a 1/α threshold written outside the agents, so neither e-values nor that threshold are claimed here; a statistic computed outside the agents also appears in Qu, Chen, Wang (pp. 1, 10, 11), Sargsyan ("Structural Enforcement of Statistical Rigor in AI-Driven Discovery: A Functional Architecture", arXiv:2511.06701v4, 2026 (first version 2025), pp. 8, 9, 11), CONFIRM (Wei et al., "CONFIRM: Governing Scientific Reporting for Trustworthy Agentic Neuroimaging", arXiv:2611.01449v2, 2026, p. 3), CADS (Chen et al., "CADS: Measuring the Incremental Contribution of LLM Proposers in Sequential Biological Screens", arXiv:2610.24597v1, 2026, p. 5), Wang et al. ("N×N E-valuation: Testing Scope-Level and Unit-Matched Predictive Associations", arXiv:2608.06621v5, 2026, pp. 1, 3) and Zhang ("Hard to Fool: A Bounded LLM Research Loop for Quantitative Finance, and What Two Dozen Campaigns Found", arXiv:2611.06607v1, 2026, pp. 1, 3–4).
+- *A plan sealed by hash before the data, and a hash-sealed record.* CONFIRM hashes its plan before any outcome is read (p. 3); Zhang hashes the campaign contract before the first proposal is evaluated, can bind a specification and its verdict code by SHA-256 for campaigns whose data do not yet exist, keeps a hash-chained ledger, and replays quotes only from a hash-verified artefact with no fallback price source (pp. 3–4); Masked Erosion (So, Liu, Long, "Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents", arXiv:2610.38392v1, 2026) freezes and hashes the authors' predictions and analysis code before each round, three later sets being frozen after related outcomes (pp. 4, 24); Qu, Chen, Wang state that order in principle (pp. 2, 10). Seeding the fresh draws by the hash of the commitment (section 2, step 7) is described here as an implementation choice.
+- *Multiple comparisons, including the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure for e-values.* Qu, Chen, Wang (pp. 2, 4, 8); Sargsyan, with an online false discovery rate (pp. 1, 15–16, 23, 25); CADS (pp. 4–6); Wang et al., on 285 hypotheses written by models (pp. 25–26); CONFIRM (pp. 5–6, 14); Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut ("Valid Stopping in Adaptive Generator–Verifier Loops", arXiv:2610.06432v1, 2026, pp. 2–3).
+- *The cheaters bench, the preregistered validation and the mutation bench (section 3.1)* are instruments of this system, and their sizes and numbers are reported as its measurements, not as ideas. An adaptive analyst who fools a reused holdout set, and an attack through the scores a leaderboard returns, and nothing else, have been prior art since 2015 (Dwork, Feldman, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Roth, "Generalization in Adaptive Data Analysis and Holdout Reuse", arXiv:1506.02629; Blum and Hardt, "The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions", arXiv:1502.04585). Qu, Chen, Wang already run an attacking controller (hidden retries) against their e-value referee (pp. 16, 29), preregister their design, with dated amendments (pp. 8, 10, 14, 29, 36), and test three deliberately leaky referees (p. 13); Masked Erosion stresses acceptance gates in the spirit of mutation testing: each generation deletes one safeguard of the agent, and the authors record whether the gate lets it through (pp. 2, 8); what is mutated is the object the gate judges, not the gate; PACE (Shawn, "PACE: Anytime-Valid Acceptance Tests for Self-Evolving Agents", arXiv:2606.08106v1, 2026) runs what it calls a unit test for acceptors (p. 6); Sargsyan uses adversarial generations (pp. 27–29) and CADS adversarial contamination channels (pp. 9, 23).
+- *The Goodhart attack (section 4)* rests on the same 2015 principle (Dwork et al.; Blum and Hardt, above). CONFIRM already serves its judge's diagnostics to a language-model proposer on 150 controls designed to be negative, with no candidate admitted (pp. 6, 9, 22); Qu, Chen, Wang name the threat (pp. 6, 18, 25) and include the referee's diagnostics in the state of the proposing model (p. 12); REUSE (Sun, Zeng, She, Wang, "Which Self-Improvements Should We Trust? Reliable Self-Improvement When Agents Reuse Their Benchmarks", arXiv:2609.33180v2, 2026) proves that a theoretical proposer that sees only the gate's decisions can force a false promotion against a class of threshold gates (p. 32); PACE studies a deployment with no planted gain, without an attack (pp. 6–7); Sargsyan gives an adversarial instruction to a language model that knows the harness, aimed at the data boundary rather than at the statistic (pp. 27–28); Yang and Shi ("Selecting LLM-Generated Hypotheses Raises Truth Beyond What an LLM Panel Expects: A Pre-registered Study on NAS-Bench-201 and HPOBench", arXiv:2611.00643v1, 2026) optimise a harness for a language-model judge's score and see that score rise while precision falls (pp. 1, 2, 10), a precedent for the premise that a language-model judge can be optimised against; Hegazy, Jordan, Dieuleveut study a generator that adapts to its verifier (pp. 1–2). The instrument of section 4, as the public code builds it (`orchestrator/pole/attaque_goodhart_v2.py`): on toy worlds where every promotion is false by construction, a language-model proposer told that it is rewarded if its law is promoted by the statistical judge (it is not told that a promotion would be false), given the judge's exact description as text and writing its laws in a closed grammar, with one arm that receives the judge's verdicts and one that does not, compared; for the candidate null v9, one graded world at its declared frontier, where a promotion is not false by construction, was added.
+- *The judge's null.* The judge promotes a hypothesis only if its prediction, sealed before the outcomes are read, beats, through an e-process computed outside the agents, the best fitted combination of the already known laws, including the organism's own archive ("nothing beyond the archive"). Judging a discovery against what is already known rather than against no effect is established practice (Feng, Giglio, Xiu, "Taming the Factor Zoo: A Test of New Factors", NBER Working Paper 25481, 2019, pp. 2–4), already applied to hypotheses proposed by language models (Liu, Liu, Liu, Mei, "Can AI Do Financial Research? LLM-Guided Hypothesis Discovery in Asset Pricing", SSRN 6569258, version dated April 2026, pp. 1, 4, 26, 28; Pan, Ding, Giesecke, "Agentic Empirical Asset Pricing: Methodological Foundations", arXiv:2609.00731v1, 2026, pp. 6, 7, 16, 25), and an e-process against the best fitted member of the current theory is published for a learned proposer (Ma et al., "Learning to Outgrow a Theory: Experimental Discovery Beyond the Initial Hypothesis Space", arXiv:2610.07627v1, 2026, pp. 1, 4, 15).
